    "customs": {
      "currencyLimits": {
        "text": "The UK government says there are no restrictions on bringing in or taking out foreign currency, but you must declare it at customs, and that importing or exporting North Korean won (KPW) is illegal. It says shops and restaurants usually charge foreign nationals in foreign currency, and some hotels may insist on full cash payment in advance.",
        "source": {
          "url": "https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/north-korea/entry-requirements",
          "name": "UK Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office — North Korea travel advice: entry requirements",
          "type": "government",
          "lastVerified": "2026-10-03",
          "lastChanged": "2026-10-03"
        }
      },
      "notes": [
        "The UK government advises against bringing books or other written material in Korean, including anything religious, and says customs confiscate literature or media deemed anti-government, subversive or pornographic. Canada's travel advice says the same applies to material printed in South Korea.",
        "The UK government says foreign mobile phones must be registered at the border and cannot connect to any network with an international SIM card, and GPS devices must be left with customs until you leave. Canada's travel advice says satellite phones and shortwave radios are confiscated on entry and usually returned on departure.",
        "Ask your guide's permission before taking any photograph, the UK government advises; Canada's travel advice says photos or videos taken outside tourist areas can lead to confiscation of your equipment or detention."
      ]
    }
