  "faq": [
    {
      "id": "need-visa",
      "question": "Do I need a visa for North Korea?",
      "answer": "Yes, for every passport whose rule we found on an official page: the UK, Canada, Germany, France, Spain, Italy, the Netherlands, Austria, the Czech Republic, Estonia, Serbia, Türkiye and Singapore each say their own ordinary-passport holders need a visa, and China says its citizens with ordinary private passports need a visa or a tourist card; no official source we could read states the rule for other passports. The UK government says tourists can normally travel only as part of an organised tour, that independent travellers need a sponsor and permission from the Ministry for Foreign Affairs, and that the borders have been closed to general entry since 2020 with only limited tourism restarted. US passports are invalid for travel there unless specially validated."
    },
    {
      "id": "us-citizens",
      "question": "Can US citizens visit North Korea?",
      "answer": "Not on a US passport unless the State Department specially validates it. The restriction comes from the US side: on 1 September 2017 US passports were declared invalid for travel to, in or through North Korea, the restriction has been extended every year since, and the latest notice runs it until 31 August 2027."
    },
    {
      "id": "open-2026",
      "question": "Is North Korea open to tourists in 2026?",
      "answer": "Barely. The UK government says North Korea's borders have been closed to general entry since 2020 and that only limited tourism has restarted."
    },
    {
      "id": "independent-travel",
      "question": "Can I travel independently in North Korea?",
      "answer": "Normally not. Canada's travel advice says you must be accompanied by an official guide at all times, and the UK government says the guide decides where you can go. The UK Foreign Office notes that tourists can normally travel only on an organised tour and that independent travellers need a sponsor and permission from the Ministry for Foreign Affairs."
    },
    {
      "id": "money",
      "question": "What money do tourists use in North Korea?",
      "answer": "Mostly cash. The UK government says credit cards and international mobile payments are not accepted, that the US dollar and the euro are the most widely accepted currencies with Chinese yuan taken in some places, and that importing or exporting the won is illegal. Canada's travel advice says cash is the most accepted form of payment and there are no ATMs, though some hotels accept credit cards if you give them advance notice."
    }
  ]
